Reviewer(s)' Comments to Author:\
\
Referee: 1\
\
Comments to the Author\
I see that Section 4 has been substantially changed, apparently in response to an error that I did not notice in my original review of this paper (sorry about that!). I have a few suggestions on the present version.\
\
## Construction of the universal equivalence\
\
Returning to this manuscript some months later, I am now finding the diagrammatic discussion on page 23 to be very challengingly worded (I grant that this part hasn\'92t changed much). In particular, it took me a very long time to guess what it means for E1\\simeq E2 to be the pullback of epsilon along Equiv -> [\\dot\{U\}_1,\\dot\{U\}_2]. The latter two maps do not form a co-span, so there is no way to speak of a pullback.\
\
My guess for what all this means is: E1 and E2 are the pullbacks of \\dot\{U\}\'92_1 and \\dot\{U\}\'92_2 (which are in the slice over [\\dot\{U\}_1,\\dot\{U\}_2]) over Equiv -> [\\dot\{U\}_1,\\dot\{U\}_2]. Then the equivalence E1 \\simeq E2 is the equivalence induced under this restriction by the universal arrow \\epsilon. My feeling was that this part of the discussion was somewhat obfuscated, and the problem is not so much the use of diagrams (which I like) but terseness and the haphazard naming of things. For example, one might have said something like \'93E1 is the domain of the universal equivalence\'94, and similarly elsewhere for the awkwardly named \\dot\{U\}'_1, etc.\
\
Maybe one thing that would help here is to not refer to Equiv -> U*U as the "universal equivalence" but rather as the "equivalence classifier". Then the actual universal equivalence is E1 \\simeq E2.\
\
## The 1-category of classified polynomials\
\
The revision describes a 1-category of \'91classified\'92 polynomials on E, where the objects are essentially the classifying maps of type families and the morphisms are cartesian squares between the families that commute strictly with the classifying maps.\
\
The original version had described the same 1-category, but introduced a notion of 2-cell determined by automorphisms on the total spaces of the families. I am not certain what exactly went wrong in this version, but I can see that it is pretty complicated, and good if it can be avoided.\
\
Now, the discussion here still seems a little overwrought: it doesn\'92t seem to me that anything is gained over simply referring to the slice E/U. It could perhaps be helpful to clarify what is gained by discussing the (strict) image of E/U in PolyFun(E,E)_cart on pages 24-25; since no 2-dimensional structure is being imposed anymore, it seems to me that one really does want to work directly with E/U. }